# Agent Conduct — private/

- This tree is the development cycle as known on ONE machine. It is not hidden from a
  session here: it is the working memory of this machine. But it is not company-wide.
- **One sanctioned crossing into `public/`:** what a change ACHIEVED may be distilled into
  the public feature log (`public/<catalog>/<Product>/features/`): drafted at close-out in
  the journal entry that accompanies its PR body here, and appended when the pull request
  merges, because a change that has not merged is not yet true. How the work was run (plan,
  wave, phase, sprint, kickoff, epic, retro, who asked for what) never crosses. The only
  delivery trace `public/` may carry is one any teammate can find in the codebase itself: a
  MERGED pull request of the product's repository.
- Nothing else here is copied or paraphrased into `public/` without the owner saying so in
  the current session. An owner RULING found inside a record here is lifted verbatim into a
  public rulings leaf, by decision, never by drift.
- Links run one way: leaves here link into `public/` freely; a `public/` leaf never links
  to, or cites the path of, anything here.
- `plans/`, `kickoffs/`, `prs/`, `appendices/` and `journal/retros/` are immutable
  provenance, exactly as `public/**/history/` is: records are added verbatim with a manifest
  row and never edited once landed.
- This is a git repository of its own: trunk `main` only, ONE commit per filing action
  (leaf + row + tags), plan and PR vocabulary welcome in subjects, history never rewritten,
  `landed/<date>-<slug>` tags mark landings. Commit as the work is done.
- **Commits here are path-scoped, always**, because this tree may have more than one
  writer: `git add <new files>` first (a pathspec commit refuses an untracked file), then
  `git commit -m "…" -- <paths>`. Never `git add -A` or `git add .`: it sweeps in another
  writer's uncommitted edits.
